# Larkon Dashboard — Visual System & Functional Implementation Contract

Reference: https://techzaa.in/larkon/admin/index.html  
Audited: 22 October 2026, Asia/Dhaka  
Audience: an AI coding agent working inside an existing dashboard repository.  
Goal: reproduce Larkon's visual system and applicable UI interactions throughout the target dashboard, using the target application's real modules, data and backend.

## 0. Read this first — execution instruction

Read the whole specification, then inspect the existing repository and implement it. This is an instruction to perform a dashboard theme migration, not merely write a plan. Preserve the target framework, routes, SSR, authentication, permissions, APIs, data models and business workflows. Replace conflicting old presentation styles throughout the dashboard, including shared navigation, cards, tables, forms, buttons, badges, menus, dialogs, charts and states. Reuse shared components rather than separately styling every page.

Use the target application's own name, logo, modules, users, permissions, language, currency and content. **Do not create Inventory, Products, Orders or other modules solely because they appear in Larkon's demo.** Map the visual patterns to modules already present. A missing target business function is not permission to invent a backend. Preserve all working actions and connect styled controls to existing handlers.

Reproduce the reference's orange/light/dark design exactly by default; do not silently substitute a previously used blue/purple theme, square cards, alternate fonts or a generic admin layout. Dashboard content and logo remain the target's. The previous storefront specification is not this dashboard's design authority.

Footer project requirement: **Developed by TaiefLab** linked to **https://taieflab.com**, consistently across dashboard pages. This replaces the demo footer's vendor credit in the target implementation and is an explicit project adaptation. Preserve any required third-party license notices in source distribution independently.

Do not run migrations, weaken auth, restart production, change production ports or deploy as part of a visual theme migration without separately granted authorization. Work on reversible code changes and produce a reviewable implementation with validation.

## 1. Evidence, scope and parity limits

### What was collected

- Source HTML for **118 unique linked Larkon demo pages**, all with a Larkon title, including business-page templates, Base UI, Advanced UI, forms, tables, charts, icons, maps, applications and authentication layouts.
- The shared `app.min.css`, `vendor.min.css`, `icons.min.css`, theme configuration, application JS, dashboard chart definitions, page/component initializers and same-directory dependencies declared by those pages. 189 unique resource references were processed; third-party references and the out-of-directory form-wizard path were skipped by the source collector, not represented as successfully downloaded.
- Exact CSS variables and computed desktop values. Desktop viewport was 1363 × 936 CSS pixels; document content width excluding scrollbar was 1348px.
- Browser inspection of the dashboard, product list, product create, button showcase, modal showcase, Grid.js tables and basic forms.
- Directly exercised: sidebar submenu expansion, light → dark → light switch, settings offcanvas, condensed sidebar, modal opening and Grid.js keyword filtering. Grid.js pagination/sorting controls and source definitions were inspected; immediate text capture after some clicks did not independently prove the completed result, so full pagination/sort behavior remains a target QA gate.

### What is not claimed

This is not a claim that every control on all 118 pages was clicked. Mobile layouts were examined through CSS and JavaScript breakpoints, not a rendered mobile viewport. CRUD persistence, payments, login, file uploads, email sending, role enforcement and all target APIs were not tested on the vendor demo. The public template has many placeholder links (`#!`, `#`, `javascript:void(0)`), static records and client-only interactions. Demo availability does not prove production functionality.

Some icons, charts and the map were missing/incomplete in the inspected browser state. Reproduce the **source-defined intended components**, not blank icon slots, blank charts, transient placeholders or the demo's technical-difficulty alert. Verify assets in the target and provide proper loading/error states. Do not claim pixel-identical completion until the target passes the final visual comparison matrix.

### Evidence precedence

1. Final computed style in a stable state at a stated viewport.
2. Shared CSS with media/theme/state selectors and final cascade order.
3. Page markup and source configuration.
4. Deliberate target integration choices, explicitly labeled.

The source appendix is a lookup aid, not a stylesheet to paste globally. Some rules are state-specific and earlier declarations are later overridden. Read selector context and resolve variables before applying.

## 2. Reference architecture and dependency strategy

The source embeds **Bootstrap 5.3.3** styling, with custom Larkon CSS, **Play** body typography, **Hanken Grotesk** headings, **Solar** icons through `iconify-icon`, and **Boxicons** glyphs. Feature-specific integrations include Bootstrap components, SimpleBar, Choices, Inputmask, Flatpickr, Quill, Dropzone, Grid.js, ApexCharts, jsVectorMap, FullCalendar, Swiper, SweetAlert, Toastify, ClipboardJS, Prism/Gumshoe and Dragula where referenced.

This is a description of reference dependencies, not a requirement to install every package. In React/Next.js, use controlled components or appropriate React adapters; in Django/templates, reuse existing progressive enhancement. Avoid running Bootstrap JS and another dialog/menu library on the same element. Do not load an entire vendor bundle merely to obtain one input or icon. Pin compatible versions using the target lockfile and verify current package documentation when implementing; retain licenses. Fetching a public preview is not proof of ownership of all commercial distribution assets.

Do not use direct DOM mutation (`innerHTML`, global event scans, class removal on route transitions) as the application architecture in a reactive framework. Reproduce user-visible behavior through scoped state and lifecycle cleanup. Lazy-load heavy charts/editors/calendar modules where used, without losing server-rendered labels, tables or authorization checks.

